1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,514
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,514 --> 00:00:03,920
In this lecture we study Introduction to Probability.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:06,490
Generated with course2video (prototype).

4
00:00:07,090 --> 00:00:08,287
Here is the plan.

5
00:00:08,287 --> 00:00:14,627
We will go through 6 parts: Introduction, Sample spaces and events, Axioms of probability,

6
00:00:14,627 --> 00:00:20,755
Conditional probability and independence, Bayes' theorem and Discrete random variables.

7
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:24,234
Let's start with part 1: Introduction.

8
00:00:24,834 --> 00:00:30,867
This course is a first introduction to probability, written with science and engineering students

9
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:31,365
in mind.

10
00:00:31,365 --> 00:00:36,216
We begin with the basic language of the subject: sample spaces and events, and

11
00:00:36,216 --> 00:00:39,140
the axioms that every probability must satisfy.

12
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:44,986
From there we move on to conditional probability, independence, and Bayes' theorem, which tell

13
00:00:44,986 --> 00:00:48,780
us how to update probabilities when we learn new information.

14
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:54,067
Later we turn to discrete random variables and two of their most important summaries,

15
00:00:54,067 --> 00:00:55,622
expectation and variance.

16
00:00:55,622 --> 00:01:00,473
To keep things concrete, each chapter ends with a short worked example, so you

17
00:01:00,473 --> 00:01:04,703
can see the ideas applied to an actual calculation before moving on.

18
00:01:05,303 --> 00:01:09,506
Now we move on to part 2: Sample spaces and events.

19
00:01:10,106 --> 00:01:15,682
With that roadmap in mind, let's start with the most basic building blocks, sample

20
00:01:15,682 --> 00:01:16,906
spaces and events.

21
00:01:16,906 --> 00:01:22,959
A random experiment is any process whose outcome cannot be predicted with certainty, such

22
00:01:22,959 --> 00:01:26,971
as tossing a coin or measuring how long a light bulb lasts.

23
00:01:26,971 --> 00:01:31,800
The sample space, which we call S, is the set of all possible outcomes.

24
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:35,948
For a single roll of a die, S is the set containing one, two,

25
00:01:35,948 --> 00:01:37,716
three, four, five and six.

26
00:01:37,716 --> 00:01:40,980
An event is simply a subset of the sample space.

27
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:45,469
For example, the event "the roll is even" is A equals the set two,

28
00:01:45,469 --> 00:01:46,149
four, six.

29
00:01:46,149 --> 00:01:50,297
We say an event occurs when the actual outcome belongs to it.

30
00:01:50,297 --> 00:01:54,990
The empty set can never occur, so it is the impossible event, while S

31
00:01:54,990 --> 00:01:58,322
itself always occurs, so it is the certain event.

32
00:01:58,922 --> 00:02:03,186
Now that we know what events are, we can combine them to build new

33
00:02:03,186 --> 00:02:03,509
ones.

34
00:02:03,509 --> 00:02:06,934
The union of A and B is the event that A or B occurs,

35
00:02:06,934 --> 00:02:08,808
meaning at least one of them.

36
00:02:08,808 --> 00:02:12,491
The intersection of A and B is the event that both occur.

37
00:02:12,491 --> 00:02:16,045
The complement of A is the event that A does not occur.

38
00:02:16,045 --> 00:02:21,924
Two events are called mutually exclusive, or disjoint, if they cannot occur together, which

39
00:02:21,924 --> 00:02:24,121
means their intersection is empty.

40
00:02:24,121 --> 00:02:25,801
Let's see this with a die.

41
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:29,226
Take A to be "the roll is even" and B to be "the roll

42
00:02:29,226 --> 00:02:30,712
is greater than three".

43
00:02:30,712 --> 00:02:35,493
Their union is the set two, four, five, six, and their intersection is the

44
00:02:35,493 --> 00:02:39,952
set four, six, the outcomes that are both even and larger than three.

45
00:02:40,552 --> 00:02:44,453
Now we move on to part 3: Axioms of probability.

46
00:02:45,053 --> 00:02:50,114
Having described events, we now need a way to attach numbers to them, and

47
00:02:50,114 --> 00:02:53,164
that brings us to the axioms of probability.

48
00:02:53,164 --> 00:02:57,671
A probability measure assigns a number, P of A, to every event A.

49
00:02:57,671 --> 00:03:01,900
Kolmogorov's axioms say what rules these numbers must follow.

50
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:07,099
First, probabilities are never negative, so P of A is greater than or equal

51
00:03:07,099 --> 00:03:07,654
to zero.

52
00:03:07,654 --> 00:03:12,577
Second, the probability of the whole sample space is one: P of S equals

53
00:03:12,577 --> 00:03:15,627
one, since the certain event always happens.

54
00:03:15,627 --> 00:03:19,510
Third, for mutually exclusive events, probabilities add.

55
00:03:19,510 --> 00:03:22,976
In symbols, P of A union B equals P of A plus P of

56
00:03:22,976 --> 00:03:25,957
B, whenever A intersect B is the empty set.

57
00:03:25,957 --> 00:03:31,365
In words, for disjoint events, the probability that one or the other occurs is

58
00:03:31,365 --> 00:03:33,861
just the sum of their probabilities.

59
00:03:34,461 --> 00:03:38,494
From these three simple axioms, several useful rules follow.

60
00:03:38,494 --> 00:03:42,057
The complement of A has probability one minus P of A.

61
00:03:42,057 --> 00:03:45,955
The impossible event, the empty set, has probability zero.

62
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:51,601
And for any two events, not necessarily disjoint, we have the general addition rule:

63
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:54,694
P of A union B equals P of A plus P of B minus

64
00:03:54,694 --> 00:03:55,971
P of A intersect B.

65
00:03:55,971 --> 00:04:01,684
The reason for subtracting is that outcomes in the overlap would otherwise be counted

66
00:04:01,684 --> 00:04:06,389
twice, so we add the probabilities and remove the double-counted part.

67
00:04:06,389 --> 00:04:10,893
A very common special case is when all outcomes are equally likely.

68
00:04:10,893 --> 00:04:15,666
Then the probability of an event is the number of outcomes in the event

69
00:04:15,666 --> 00:04:18,354
divided by the total number of outcomes.

70
00:04:18,354 --> 00:04:22,791
For a fair die, the probability of an even roll is three over six,

71
00:04:22,791 --> 00:04:24,001
which is one half.

72
00:04:24,601 --> 00:04:29,825
Now we move on to part 4: Conditional probability and independence.

73
00:04:30,425 --> 00:04:35,061
So far our probabilities assumed we knew nothing about the outcome.

74
00:04:35,061 --> 00:04:41,564
We now turn to conditional probability and independence, which describe how the probability of

75
00:04:41,564 --> 00:04:45,508
an event changes when we know another event has occurred.

76
00:04:45,508 --> 00:04:49,728
The probability of A given B is defined as P of A intersect B

77
00:04:49,728 --> 00:04:50,973
divided by P of B.

78
00:04:50,973 --> 00:04:56,370
In words, it is the probability that both occur, divided by the probability of

79
00:04:56,370 --> 00:04:56,508
B.

80
00:04:56,508 --> 00:05:01,075
This definition only makes sense when P of B is greater than zero.

81
00:05:01,075 --> 00:05:05,918
If we rearrange it, we get the multiplication rule: P of A intersect B

82
00:05:05,918 --> 00:05:08,339
equals P of A given B times P of B.

83
00:05:08,339 --> 00:05:13,666
This rule is often the easiest way to compute the probability that two things

84
00:05:13,666 --> 00:05:14,497
both happen.

85
00:05:15,097 --> 00:05:19,437
Building on conditional probability, we get two important tools.

86
00:05:19,437 --> 00:05:22,285
The first is the law of total probability.

87
00:05:22,285 --> 00:05:27,033
If we split the sample space into a partition of disjoint cases, B one

88
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:30,763
through B n, then P of A equals the sum, for i from one

89
00:05:30,763 --> 00:05:33,543
to n, of P of A given B i times P of B i.

90
00:05:33,543 --> 00:05:38,901
So the probability of A is a weighted sum of its conditional probabilities over

91
00:05:38,901 --> 00:05:39,850
all the cases.

92
00:05:39,850 --> 00:05:42,020
The second idea is independence.

93
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:47,921
Two events are independent if knowing that one occurred does not change the probability

94
00:05:47,921 --> 00:05:48,802
of the other.

95
00:05:48,802 --> 00:05:52,871
Equivalently, P of A intersect B equals P of A times P of B.

96
00:05:52,871 --> 00:05:57,144
Be careful: independence is not the same as mutual exclusivity.

97
00:05:57,144 --> 00:06:03,790
Disjoint events with positive probabilities are always dependent, because if one occurs, the other

98
00:06:03,790 --> 00:06:04,265
cannot.

99
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:10,347
To close this chapter, let's apply the multiplication rule to a concrete example.

100
00:06:10,347 --> 00:06:14,950
Suppose we draw two cards from a standard deck, without replacement.

101
00:06:14,950 --> 00:06:17,860
What is the probability that both are aces?

102
00:06:17,860 --> 00:06:22,057
The first card is an ace with probability four over fifty-two.

103
00:06:22,057 --> 00:06:27,471
Then we multiply by three over fifty-one, giving about zero point zero zero four

104
00:06:27,471 --> 00:06:27,810
five.

105
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:33,563
Notice that the second factor is a conditional probability: given that the first card

106
00:06:33,563 --> 00:06:38,572
was an ace, it has been removed, so only three aces remain among fifty-one

107
00:06:38,572 --> 00:06:38,978
cards.

108
00:06:38,978 --> 00:06:43,716
This is exactly the multiplication rule at work, P of both equals P of

109
00:06:43,716 --> 00:06:47,032
the second given the first, times P of the first.

110
00:06:47,632 --> 00:06:51,313
Now we move on to part 5: Bayes' theorem.

111
00:06:51,913 --> 00:06:57,919
Having worked through conditional probabilities like the two aces example, we can now turn

112
00:06:57,919 --> 00:06:59,254
the question around.

113
00:06:59,254 --> 00:07:02,591
Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional probability.

114
00:07:02,591 --> 00:07:07,397
It lets us express the probability of B given A using the probability of

115
00:07:07,397 --> 00:07:11,535
A given B, which is often the quantity that is easier to know.

116
00:07:11,535 --> 00:07:16,474
The formula says: the probability of B given A equals the probability of A

117
00:07:16,474 --> 00:07:21,346
given B, times the probability of B, all divided by the probability of A.

118
00:07:21,346 --> 00:07:26,352
In words, the posterior probability of B given A equals the likelihood of A

119
00:07:26,352 --> 00:07:29,555
given B times the prior of B, divided by P of A.

120
00:07:29,555 --> 00:07:34,161
We call P of B the prior probability, our belief before seeing A, and

121
00:07:34,161 --> 00:07:38,699
P of B given A the posterior probability, our belief after seeing A.

122
00:07:38,699 --> 00:07:43,972
The denominator, P of A, is usually computed with the law of total probability.

123
00:07:44,572 --> 00:07:48,451
To see Bayes' theorem in action, consider a medical test.

124
00:07:48,451 --> 00:07:52,262
Suppose a disease affects one percent of the population.

125
00:07:52,262 --> 00:07:58,114
The test detects ninety-nine percent of sick people, but it also gives false positives

126
00:07:58,114 --> 00:08:00,496
for five percent of healthy people.

127
00:08:00,496 --> 00:08:05,872
What is the probability that someone is actually sick, given a positive result?

128
00:08:05,872 --> 00:08:11,316
Applying Bayes, the numerator is zero point nine nine times zero point zero one.

129
00:08:11,316 --> 00:08:17,032
The denominator adds the false positives: zero point nine nine times zero point zero

130
00:08:17,032 --> 00:08:20,979
one, plus zero point zero five times zero point nine nine.

131
00:08:20,979 --> 00:08:25,947
The result is only about zero point one seven, roughly seventeen percent.

132
00:08:25,947 --> 00:08:26,696
Why so low?

133
00:08:26,696 --> 00:08:31,936
Because the prior is so small that most positive results are false positives.

134
00:08:31,936 --> 00:08:38,605
The healthy majority generates more false alarms than the sick minority generates true detections.

135
00:08:39,205 --> 00:08:43,477
Now we move on to part 6: Discrete random variables.

136
00:08:44,077 --> 00:08:49,211
So far we have worked with probabilities of events; now we attach numbers to

137
00:08:49,211 --> 00:08:49,819
outcomes.

138
00:08:49,819 --> 00:08:54,345
A random variable is a function assigning a number to each outcome.

139
00:08:54,345 --> 00:08:58,938
It is discrete when it takes a finite or countable number of values.

140
00:08:58,938 --> 00:09:04,747
Its probability mass function, written p of x, gives the probability that the variable

141
00:09:04,747 --> 00:09:08,462
equals x; these values are non-negative and sum to one.

142
00:09:08,462 --> 00:09:13,934
The expectation is a probability-weighted average: E of X equals the sum over all

143
00:09:13,934 --> 00:09:17,514
x of x times p of x, so each value is weighted by its

144
00:09:17,514 --> 00:09:18,324
probability.

145
00:09:18,324 --> 00:09:21,634
The variance measures the spread around the mean.

146
00:09:21,634 --> 00:09:26,363
A convenient formula is: the variance of X equals E of X squared minus

147
00:09:26,363 --> 00:09:27,781
the square of E of X.

148
00:09:27,781 --> 00:09:32,375
In other words, the mean of the square minus the square of the mean.

149
00:09:32,975 --> 00:09:39,007
With mean and variance defined, let's see how they behave under simple transformations.

150
00:09:39,007 --> 00:09:43,028
Expectation is linear: E of a X plus b equals a times E of

151
00:09:43,028 --> 00:09:45,593
X, plus b, for any constants a and b.

152
00:09:45,593 --> 00:09:50,932
Scaling and shifting a random variable scales and shifts its mean in the same

153
00:09:50,932 --> 00:09:51,209
way.

154
00:09:51,209 --> 00:09:55,854
For variance, the variance of a X plus b equals a squared times the

155
00:09:55,854 --> 00:09:56,825
variance of X.

156
00:09:56,825 --> 00:10:02,649
Scaling multiplies the variance by the square of the factor, while adding a constant

157
00:10:02,649 --> 00:10:05,006
does not change the spread at all.

158
00:10:05,006 --> 00:10:06,046
Now a fair die.

159
00:10:06,046 --> 00:10:09,860
E of X is one plus two up to six, divided by six, which

160
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:11,454
gives three point five.

161
00:10:11,454 --> 00:10:15,475
The expected square, E of X squared, is ninety-one sixths.

162
00:10:15,475 --> 00:10:21,299
So the variance is ninety-one sixths minus three point five squared, which is twelve

163
00:10:21,299 --> 00:10:24,627
point two five, giving about two point nine two.

164
00:10:25,227 --> 00:10:27,945
That brings us to the end of this lecture.

165
00:10:27,945 --> 00:10:35,386
We covered Introduction, Sample spaces and events, Axioms of probability, Conditional probability and independence,

166
00:10:35,386 --> 00:10:38,298
Bayes' theorem and Discrete random variables.

167
00:10:38,298 --> 00:10:39,786
Thank you for watching.
